tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Inca suspension bridges and the Qhapaq Nan", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Grass-rope suspension bridge over a gorge on the Inca road.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Inca, 15th-16th centuries; the Q'eswachaka is rewoven every year to this day", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A 40,000-km road network crossed the Andes with stairways, causeways and grass-rope", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "suspension bridges spanning 45 metres and more, maintained by labour tax (mita) and served", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "by relay runners (chasquis) and storehouses (qollqa).", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Villagers twist ichu grass into cords, cords into cables as thick as a body, and stretch", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "four floor cables and two handrail cables between stone anchors; a mat of branches makes", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "the deck. The bridge sags by design and lasts about a year.", "color": "gray"}
